a classical B2B business.

00:10:25.985 --> 00:10:28.870
And mainly restaurants,
hotels, caterers,

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:30.520
these are our
classical customers.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.410
And as I said, we're running
different business models,

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:33.880
and that makes it
extremely hard.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.950
Because we have a
central IT serving

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.830
these more than 30 countries
with different business models,

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.070
and that makes it very
complex to standardize.

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.570
But that's one of the reasons
why we focus very much on SAP,

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.153
because this is the
standard solution,

00:10:45.153 --> 00:10:47.198
and we want to simplify
our process landscape.

00:10:47.198 --> 00:10:47.990
PAUL YOUNG: Thanks.

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.193
And then Meena, McKesson is--

00:10:50.193 --> 00:10:51.860
again, McKesson's a
fascinating company.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.700
Fortune 6, sixth largest
company in America, and it's

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.090
amazing the number of people I
meet who say, who are McKesson?

00:10:58.090 --> 00:10:59.380
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Exactly.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.290
Yeah, so as you mentioned,
it's a Fortune 6 company

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:07.000
with revenues of close
to, I think, $210 billion.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:11.230
And it's been in business
for over, I think, 185 years.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.000
I don't know exact
number by now.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:14.060
Who's counting?

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:18.460
But so we primarily deliver--

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.190
basically, one-third of
prescription medicines

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:25.120
in North America are
delivered by McKesson.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:28.450
Then we also have a
pretty large offering

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:34.570
in our brand and private label
of medical/surgical supplies.

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:39.610
Then we operate in the specialty
health care space as well,

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:45.700
supporting upwards of, I
think, 9,000-plus oncologists

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:50.290
and specialists
throughout our networks.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:54.910
And then we also
support a large number

00:11:54.910 --> 00:12:00.441
of independently owned and
banner pharmacies as well.

00:12:00.441 --> 00:12:04.210
We are about, from an
employee strength perspective,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:07.670
I think we are about
78,000-plus at the moment.

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:12.910
And we operate across
16 countries right now.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:14.030
So yeah.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:14.822
So yeah, that's it.

00:12:14.822 --> 00:12:16.447
PAUL YOUNG: And again,
just to give you

00:12:16.447 --> 00:12:18.880
a perspective on McKesson, I
think even inside Google when

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.922
we started working with
McKesson, a lot of people

00:12:20.922 --> 00:12:22.240
were like, who are McKesson?

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.310
What do you do?

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.410
Because I used to call
on McKesson with my SAP

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:28.187
badge on back in the days.

00:12:28.187 --> 00:12:30.520
But the question I always put
to people, if you can just

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:31.450
imagine this, is--

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:32.635
so think about
your local hospital

00:12:32.635 --> 00:12:35.020
or your local doctor's office,
your local dental offices.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:37.030
You'll understand
all the consumables

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:40.150
they go through every day
in terms of saline bags

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:42.760
and gloves and needles
and everything they need.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:45.010
And then if you've
never questioned it,

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:47.170
wonder, why is there
not a giant warehouse

00:12:47.170 --> 00:12:48.587
attached to those hospitals?

00:12:48.587 --> 00:12:50.170
And the simple answer
is-- the reason,

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.070
because McKesson
is that warehouse.

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:54.655
I mean, for eight hours a day,
pretty much every hospital

00:12:54.655 --> 00:12:56.530
or doctor's office,
dentist office, whatever,

00:12:56.530 --> 00:13:00.490
calls McKesson and says, I
need these things tomorrow

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:01.990
or in the next 40 hours.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:02.700
And it goes out.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.638
I mean, and this
is part of the--

00:13:04.638 --> 00:13:05.680
I want to reinforce this.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:08.590
It's not just the scale of these
two customers and who they are

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:16.180
and what we're taking on
here, but also crucially,

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:19.000
where reliability and
performance are everything,

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:19.530
right?

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:22.062
If Metro stops working,
there's a lot of restaurants

00:13:22.062 --> 00:13:23.020
that just stop working.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:24.562
And there's a lot
of people, frankly,

00:13:24.562 --> 00:13:26.240
that don't eat because of it.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.710
If McKesson stops working,
operations get canceled.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:30.998
We have huge issues.

00:13:30.998 --> 00:13:32.790
Hospitals don't keep
the supplies in stock.

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.670
They look at their schedule,
and they order to your operation

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:38.120
having being scheduled.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:41.980
So when people come back and
go, wow, is the cloud ready,

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:45.010
this isn't just, hey, it's a
little side accounting system.

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:49.840
We're talking serious
economic impact and challenge

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:50.860
if we don't do it right.

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.185
This is a huge step for us.

00:13:52.185 --> 00:13:53.560
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
Absolutely.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.830
What comes to mind is
a couple of years ago,

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:59.740
we had a leadership
conference here.

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:04.110
And our CEO came to talk to us.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:09.150
And somebody asked him, what
are your topmost priorities

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:11.170
that IT can be working on?

00:14:11.170 --> 00:14:14.120
And he kind of just
looked point blank at us.

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:16.170
He's like, don't break
anything, and don't

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:18.100
let anyone steal anything.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.160
So that's our expectation
of Google as well.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:24.280
That's the table stakes for us.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.310
Yeah, because those operations--

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:30.990
excellence is a huge
part of our culture.

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:33.083
And that's very,
very important to us.

00:14:33.083 --> 00:14:34.750
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah,
"don't steal anything"

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:35.910
was in reference to the data.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:36.330
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Yes.

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.540
PAUL YOUNG: Because you also
have a huge amount of data on--

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:38.820
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
Absolutely, yeah.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:39.860
PAUL YOUNG: --people and
their medical conditions,

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:40.693
and everything else.

00:14:40.693 --> 00:14:41.250
No pressure.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:42.080
No pressure on me.

00:14:42.080 --> 00:14:44.250
And anyway, so thanks for that.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.290
Equally, the other thing
I think is fascinating

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:48.360
here is two industries,
health care and retail,

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.690
where these are not industries
you go to retire in,

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:52.965
effectively.

00:14:52.965 --> 00:14:55.650
These are industries that are
in massive change, globally.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.170
And I think that leads us into
the conversation too, which

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:03.458
is this time last year, a lot
of the conversation I got was

00:15:03.458 --> 00:15:04.750
lift and shift, lift and shift.

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.542
We just need to lift
and shift what we have

00:15:06.542 --> 00:15:07.740
and move it into the cloud.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.115
I think in both of your cases,
there is no lift and shift

00:15:10.115 --> 00:15:12.618
here.

00:15:12.618 --> 00:15:13.910
We're talking massive projects.

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.130
And along the way, your
business and your industry

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:18.830
is changing rapidly, and so you
have to deal with that change

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:19.350
as well.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:22.460
Do you want to just quickly
highlight what keeps you awake

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:23.840
at night in that context?

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:24.965
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:15:24.965 --> 00:15:29.230
Obviously, I mean, health care
is such a rapidly changing--

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:32.450
at such a rapid scale.

00:15:32.450 --> 00:15:37.640
It's becoming increasingly
high-tech and very tech-savvy.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.740
Also, our consumers
are demanding more.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:42.170
They have a lot more power.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:44.990
And then we have a large
set of aging population

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:46.940
across the globe.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:49.940
And then you have these
government agencies

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.970
and regulators
constantly changing

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:55.080
the rules and policies on us.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:59.840
So you have to be able to
quickly adapt to those changes

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:04.770
and quickly deliver
to those requirements.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:09.110
So IT can have a pretty
large, impactful role

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:13.670
to enable our business to be
able to meet those demands.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.740
So yeah, so the
change is constant,

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:17.940
and that's what we are
seeing continuously.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:20.750
And that's where
we need to focus

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:23.270
on modernizing our
architectures to be

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.302
able to meet the needs
of the business demands.

00:16:25.302 --> 00:16:26.010
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:29.630
And Timo, yeah, retail's an
easy life these days, right?

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:30.903
Not much change going on?

00:16:30.903 --> 00:16:32.070
TIMO SALZSIEDER: Absolutely.

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:32.780
No.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:35.820
In fact, we are facing
huge issues in front of us.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.010
So I think in the past,
it was quite easy.

00:16:38.010 --> 00:16:41.420
I mean, you just sold your
stuff, and you're all set.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:43.580
These days, things are
changing radically.

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:47.540
So particularly in our markets,
if you look into Asia--

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:49.952
I mean, I think a lot of
innovation out of retail

00:16:49.952 --> 00:16:51.660
is coming, in the
meantime, out of China.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:53.870
So for example, the usage
of mobile devices in China

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.453
is completely different
than in Europe

00:16:55.453 --> 00:16:57.140
and I also believe in the US.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:17:00.275
So it's pretty normal that I
know an 80-year-old woman gets

00:17:00.275 --> 00:17:02.150
to a store, and she's
having her mobile phone

00:17:02.150 --> 00:17:03.530
and paying with her
mobile phone and scanning

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:04.322
product, et cetera.

00:17:04.322 --> 00:17:05.430
So that's pretty normal.

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.670
So the speed of
innovation pushed out

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.069
of these markets and
competitors like Alibaba--

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:11.599
so long-term
competitors, or Amazon.

00:17:11.599 --> 00:17:13.641
I mean, they're pushing
hard into that direction.

00:17:13.641 --> 00:17:15.839
They bring a lot of
innovation to that space.

00:17:15.839 --> 00:17:18.829
And for us, we really try
to be an omnichannel player.

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:21.200
So therefore, we have this
brick-and-mortar business

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:22.690
with huge stores
around the globe.

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.460
And what we need to do is
obviously drive our customers

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:27.290
a bit more into this
omnichannel world.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:29.150
And this makes it
quite difficult.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:32.030
Also, coming also from
more B2C background,

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:34.280
I just figured out that
obviously, the customer

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.230
behavior is completely
different in our world.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:38.780
So it's not that customers
are searching for a product.

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:41.300
If a restaurant owner
gets into our stores,

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:43.350
he or she knows exactly
what they're looking for.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.558
So therefore, completely
different customer behavior.

00:17:45.558 --> 00:17:47.840
They don't want to spend
too much time in a store.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:50.000
So they want to, obviously,
get in and get out

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.167
as fast as possible with
the best price and the best

00:17:52.167 --> 00:17:53.230
customer experience.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:54.980
And so therefore,
optimizing, for example,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:56.560
the checkout process
at the cashier

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.180
is one of the topics we're
bringing a lot of technology

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:00.020
currently in--

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.190
so a lot of, also, AI technology
like vision APIs, et cetera,

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.078
just to detect products.

00:18:05.078 --> 00:18:06.620
Also running a lot
of innovation when

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.920
it comes to augmented realities,
so you take your mobile phone,

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.390
put it on a product,
and then you

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:13.380
get some additional
information on the product.

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.590
So this kind of topics are
brand new to our world.

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:18.140
And obviously, this
is a huge change

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:20.820
within our internal processes,
and also in the mindset.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:23.090
So when it comes
to agility and--

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:25.095
I don't know-- self-organized
teams, et cetera,

00:18:25.095 --> 00:18:25.970
these are the topics.

00:18:25.970 --> 00:18:28.050
And as I said, we're a
pretty old organization.

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:32.240
And if you want to transform the
organization into a very fast,

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:33.890
high-speed organization,
this is also

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.300
one of the challenges
that keeps me awake.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:35.800
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah, cool.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.530
We'll come back to
that one in a second.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:41.380
So I wasn't joking at the
start where I said, for me,

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.970
the answer was, let's get
the biggest customers we can,

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.367
and let's prove we can do this.

00:18:46.367 --> 00:18:48.700
Both of you represent, on the
stage, [INAUDIBLE] to SAP.

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.840
Do you want to just
give people a sense

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:54.630
on, statistically, where you are
at with your SAP installations?

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.180
TIMO SALZSIEDER:
So we are running

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:57.710
not only one SAP installation.

00:18:57.710 --> 00:18:58.760
We're running multiple.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:01.620
As I said, we have this
decentralized environment.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.770
So what we're doing currently
with SAP, and in context

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:07.300
on Google Cloud, is that we
are consolidating more than 100

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:08.960
different accounting systems.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:10.610
And you just heard
that I'm saying

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:12.830
we have 25 or 30 countries.

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.140
And you might ask yourself,
OK, 25 or 30 countries,

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.240
why we run 100 different
accounting systems?

00:19:17.240 --> 00:19:19.850
So consolidation of these
systems is quite hard for us,

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:21.350
and we expect that
this project will

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:24.150
take three to five years to
consolidate all these systems.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.360
If you look in the cost
center infrastructure,

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.527
the numbers of cost centers
we have across the globe

00:19:28.527 --> 00:19:29.480
are more than 10,000.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.438
So to consolidate them
into a couple of hundred

00:19:31.438 --> 00:19:32.580
is already a big challenge.

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:36.230
So this is where we are, and
we started this whole journey

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:37.680
about 12 months
ago where we said,

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:41.898
OK, let's look how the future
of our SAP installation will be.

00:19:41.898 --> 00:19:44.190
And we have the general
strategy to move towards cloud.

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.460
So we're doing this, obviously,
not only in SAP world,

00:19:46.460 --> 00:19:49.100
but also in our digital
initiatives when it comes

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.550
to online sales, et cetera.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.970
But now we are in the
middle of that journey,

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:55.370
and it's pretty clear
that we're moving into SAP

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:57.662
S/4HANA on Google Cloud.

00:19:57.662 --> 00:19:59.120
And this is where
we are right now.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:01.662
And yeah, project just started
about a couple of months ago.

00:20:01.662 --> 00:20:03.120
We're still in the
conceptual phase

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.700
and currently very close to
bringing the first country

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:07.080
live, and then
obviously continue

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.700
with rolling out
the solution to all

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:09.780
of the rest of the countries.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.697
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah, I mean,
it's a huge project.

00:20:11.697 --> 00:20:14.620
It's funny, when we published
an article about you,

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:16.770
and the number of phone
calls I got from SAP people

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.455
and customers who came
back and said, wait,

00:20:19.455 --> 00:20:20.610
it has to be a typo.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:24.020
That article says there are
more than 100 individual SAP

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:24.520
systems.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.437
Historically, it was
completely decentralized.

00:20:26.437 --> 00:20:28.170
And they're
consolidating it to one.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:30.087
And for the record,
there's only two customers

00:20:30.087 --> 00:20:31.712
I know of globally
that are doing this.

00:20:31.712 --> 00:20:32.540
You're one of them.

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.832
There's another one that's
not public who's doing this.

00:20:34.832 --> 00:20:36.940
But I mean, it is a--

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:38.460
you mean, with all
the stuff we just

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:41.040
talked about changing
your industry,

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:43.590
the complexity that's involved
in doing all of those changes

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:44.882
and bringing them all together.

00:20:44.882 --> 00:20:46.740
And inevitably, I think--

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:50.430
maybe I'm wrong, but my
perspective on your project,

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:53.250
for a lot of people,
you start this project

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:54.160
with a destination.

00:20:54.160 --> 00:20:56.590
It's unlikely to be the actual
destination you end up at,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:57.090
right?

00:20:57.090 --> 00:20:58.780
Because the world's going
to change in the meantime.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:01.080
And so a lot of what
hopefully we're doing for you

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.535
and a lot of what you need
is the flexibility, right?

00:21:03.535 --> 00:21:07.520
It's the understanding that
there is no set destination,

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:10.130
and the ability
to change rapidly

00:21:10.130 --> 00:21:11.760
at all layers in
the stack is what's

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:12.960
going to get you where
you need to get to.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:13.840
Is that that fair?

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:14.040
TIMO SALZSIEDER: Yeah.

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:16.795
So scalability, flexibility-- so
these are the keywords for us.

00:21:16.795 --> 00:21:19.170
Obviously, we're also interested
in nobody stealing stuff

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:20.190
from us.

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.470
But frankly, this is a core
requirement on our side.

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.480
So we really want
to do much more.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.220
And therefore, Google is, for
us, not only a strategic part

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:28.860
when it comes to our
finance SAP role.

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.580
It's also an overall
strategic partner.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.180
So long-term, we plan to
move basically everything

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:33.850
into the cloud.

00:21:33.850 --> 00:21:35.490
So that's a clear strategy here.

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:37.260
And therefore, there's
a high demand also

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.550
when it comes to cultural
aspects, collaboration,

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.777
innovation, these kind of
topics with SAP and Google.

00:21:41.777 --> 00:21:43.860
And therefore, we're pushing
hard on these topics.

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:44.620
But you're right.

00:21:44.620 --> 00:21:45.807
It's a very complex process.

00:21:45.807 --> 00:21:47.640
This will take, as I
said, a couple of years

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.410
to finalize that one.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:51.532
And the biggest challenge
is not only technology,

00:21:51.532 --> 00:21:53.740
also convince the people
that is the right way to go.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.198
So I have a lot of CFOs sitting
in the countries telling me

00:21:56.198 --> 00:21:57.615
that this is a
pretty stupid idea,

00:21:57.615 --> 00:21:59.760
that they want to sit on
their existing processes

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:00.840
and infrastructure.

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:02.550
And therefore, we
need to convince also

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:04.290
these kind of people,
making sure that everybody's

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:05.290
following the same path.

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:07.320
But standardization
and harmonization

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:09.110
and simplification of
the processes, this

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:10.820
is a clear target of Metro.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:14.070
PAUL YOUNG: Meena, you want
to run the statistics package

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:15.362
on SAP for McKesson?

00:22:15.362 --> 00:22:17.070
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
For McKesson, do I?

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:18.360
Yeah.

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:23.190
Yeah, it's considered
as, I think,

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:26.050
amongst the top three or
five, or something like that.

00:22:26.050 --> 00:22:28.170
So it's a pretty
large install base

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:33.400
that we have of SAP across
Europe and North America.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:37.200
We're probably close
to, I want to say, maybe

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:40.590
30-plus products that
are either deployed

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:44.220
or on their way to be deployed.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:50.220
And probably close to
upwards of 260 SAP instances

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.230
that we have, and I'm sure
every day, the number changes.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:54.390
And one of our largest--

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.550
PAUL YOUNG: 260
production systems?

00:22:56.550 --> 00:22:58.800
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: No,
sorry, non-production, but--

00:22:58.800 --> 00:22:59.350
PAUL YOUNG: Non-production, OK.

00:22:59.350 --> 00:23:00.270
Cool.

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.160
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
But I'll talk

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.470
about why the non-production
is also important.

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:09.360
Because in North America,
one of our ECC instances

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:10.980
is upwards of 100 terabytes.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.100
And when you think about the
entire landscape-- and most

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:17.050
of them, except for development,
is a copy of production.

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:18.780
So you're talking big-scale.

00:23:18.780 --> 00:23:20.820
And if you're
talking about cloud,

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:22.340
I mean, that's
just not going to--

00:23:22.340 --> 00:23:27.870
that's just impossible
to move, lift, and shift.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:34.290
And also, that system currently
supports transaction volumes

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:36.510
close to-- if I think
about peak lines,

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:42.900
it's, I want to say, close to
2 million line items a day.

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.690
And that too, most
of the processing

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:50.580
is done within the four
to six-hour peak time.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:54.300
And then availability,
I think it's almost a 24

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:58.770
by 7 requirement,
which we're not

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.560
achieving that because we
do have some maintenance

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:03.480
windows on the weekend,
a very short one,

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:05.820
which now the business, we're
getting a lot of pressure

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:07.770
to eliminate that.

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:09.930
So we will be
looking to Google--

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:11.250
PAUL YOUNG: [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.480
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
--to help us with that.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:16.710
And then we're also very,
very large HANA shop.

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:21.000
We've had HANA deployed
in our environment,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:22.890
I think, since 2014.

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:26.430
It's probably one of the
largest BW and HANA system

00:24:26.430 --> 00:24:27.390
that we have.

00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:30.120
It's 24 terabytes.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:32.820
And a bunch of other HANA
systems, and we also--

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:36.260
transactional systems, I should
say, that's a suite on HANA.

00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:41.070
So the point being that
we do have a good amount

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:44.130
of talent in HANA skills.

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.580
And then as we look
at our S/4 journey

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:54.110
right now, where that started,
for the UK implementation,

00:24:54.110 --> 00:24:57.220
that's a completely
greenfield implementation.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:25:00.420
So that's starting on GCP.

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.730
It has already started on GCP.

00:25:03.730 --> 00:25:05.950
When it comes to
North America, I

00:25:05.950 --> 00:25:11.950
think we have to see, get the
experience with getting our UK

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:14.210
environment on GCP
and learn from that,

00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:17.620
and then take those learnings
and bring them back and plan

00:25:17.620 --> 00:25:19.420
our journey here.

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:21.850
Yeah, so that's where
we are just mostly--

00:25:21.850 --> 00:25:24.800
North America, mostly
forming norming stage.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:26.332
UK, we've started.

00:25:26.332 --> 00:25:27.790
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah,
it's fascinating.

00:25:27.790 --> 00:25:29.832
Because for both of you,
the ultimate destination

00:25:29.832 --> 00:25:31.832
is the same, which is
effectively, in your case,

00:25:31.832 --> 00:25:33.340
one giant S/4
system; in your case,

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:35.500
probably two-- one for
Europe, and one for America.

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:39.970
But the two journeys are
just radically different,

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:42.080
and fascinating as it goes.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.410
So I think one of the things--

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:45.910
obviously, there's
a bunch of people

00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:49.180
in the audience who
ultimately are either are

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:51.595
on the same journey you're
on or are considering

00:25:51.595 --> 00:25:52.720
the same journey you're on.

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:56.348
So I think that's a little
bit of this, which is just

00:25:56.348 --> 00:25:57.640
a couple of specific questions.

00:25:57.640 --> 00:26:01.555
But one of them is, how do you
actually set about evaluating?

00:26:01.555 --> 00:26:03.430
At the point where it
comes to the, OK, we've

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:05.140
got to pick a
cloud provider, how

00:26:05.140 --> 00:26:08.280
did you set about evaluating
Google Cloud specifically,

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:10.647
doubtless others as well?

00:26:10.647 --> 00:26:12.230
How do you set that
decision criteria?

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:13.540
How do you get that
process together?

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:14.440
What did you learn from that?

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:14.920
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
Yeah, I think

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:17.050
there's a lot of factors
that went into that.

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:20.010
We have been on a cloud
journey for a while, actually,

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:22.810
with some other providers.

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:26.860
But essentially, it
all came down to what

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:29.050
Google brought to the table.

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:32.860
You may have heard our CTO talk
in the keynote this morning--

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:39.850
the software engineering
mindset and a mindset of, hey,

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:42.490
challenging us to actually
think differently.

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:45.280
Because previously, we were
all thinking lift and shift,

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:49.850
and total cost of ownership
optimization, and all of that.

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:52.630
And we were really not
moving the dial forward.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:56.320
So with Google coming in and
challenging us on that front

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:58.900
and showing that partnership.

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:02.860
And then the other
side is SAP, the scale

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.220
that I just talked about.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:07.180
I mean, McKesson,
everything else around it

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:10.250
will move if SAP moves.

00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:12.870
So to be able to--

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:17.950
so that became one of the big
use cases for the evaluation.

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:22.190
So last year, we did
a POC with Google.

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:25.915
And we were, within a
four to six weeks' time

00:27:25.915 --> 00:27:30.070
frame, able to actually set
up SAP system, soup to nuts,

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:33.620
and test a lot of our
operational capabilities there.

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:36.660
So that became actually--

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:40.150
I am pretty sure that became
also one of the major inputs

00:27:40.150 --> 00:27:42.350
back into the
decision-making process.

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:44.740
I'm sure there's a lot
more that went into it,

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:47.398
but I will say that this
was a pretty big use case.

00:27:47.398 --> 00:27:48.940
PAUL YOUNG: I think
it's interesting.

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:50.560
I mean, if I net it
out, fundamentally,

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:52.643
the feedback from McKesson
at the end of it is you

00:27:52.643 --> 00:27:54.410
guys entered this
with the question,

00:27:54.410 --> 00:27:55.990
do you have the boxes?

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:58.930
And we exited with the question
of, do you have the processes?

00:27:58.930 --> 00:27:59.380
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Right.

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:00.730
PAUL YOUNG: And
that's the difference.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:01.570
Timo, do you have a--

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:02.830
TIMO SALZSIEDER:
Yeah, we're coming

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:03.872
from a different journey.

00:28:03.872 --> 00:28:07.760
So we're pushing very hard, as I
said, this omnichannel journey.

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:09.530
So we started about
two years ago--

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:11.140
actually, three years ago--

00:28:11.140 --> 00:28:13.390
this way, just to move
into the delivery business.

00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:15.760
So food delivery business is
extremely relevant for us.

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.680
We're growing two-digit percent
every year now since then.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.890
So we are currently
generating more than 6 billion

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:22.400
already out of
delivery business--

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:25.780
so quite strong growth over
the last two, three years.

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:27.920
And scalability is a
clear issue for us.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:30.790
So for example, if you are
active in a country like India,

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:32.140
and you push the wrong
button, and you send out

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:32.830
a campaign to--

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:34.590
I don't know-- 20,
30 million people,

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:37.480
then obviously, you have a
scalability question to solve.

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:39.970
And therefore, it was pretty
clear that this cannot be done

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:41.460
with our existing
own data centers.

00:28:41.460 --> 00:28:43.830
So we decided, OK, let's
move into the cloud space.

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:46.480
We were looking into that area,
and just, as you mentioned,

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:48.550
the engineering perspective
is extremely relevant for us.

00:28:48.550 --> 00:28:50.467
And obviously, I think
it's pretty clear, just

00:28:50.467 --> 00:28:52.202
looking into this event here--

00:28:52.202 --> 00:28:54.160
I mean, the engineers,
they simply love Google.

00:28:54.160 --> 00:28:57.070
So that was already kind of a
commitment from the engineering

00:28:57.070 --> 00:28:57.970
perspective.

00:28:57.970 --> 00:29:00.370
But cost played a role
also, and how are we

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.090
treated when it
comes, for example,

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:04.300
to the negotiations, et cetera.

00:29:04.300 --> 00:29:07.160
Because this is, at least for me
personally, extremely relevant.

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:08.735
So Google did a pretty good job.

00:29:08.735 --> 00:29:11.110
And therefore, we made the
decision, about two years ago,

00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.750
to move our digital
products into Google Cloud,

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.120
mainly due to scalability
and also global scale,

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:19.870
although, just to be very
frank, we have some issues here,

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:22.480
obviously, with China and with
Russia because Google is not

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.170
present in these countries
at this point, at least.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.690
So we hope that something is
moving there, hopefully soon.

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:29.140
But so the announcement
yesterday with [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:30.190
helps us a lot.

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:31.725
Because if, for example--

00:29:31.725 --> 00:29:33.850
I don't know-- we could
partner with a public cloud

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:35.590
provider in China,
and then there

00:29:35.590 --> 00:29:38.397
is a nice link using
[INAUDIBLE] into that

00:29:38.397 --> 00:29:40.480
cloud environment helps,
obviously, our operation.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.290
So therefore, this was
the starting point.

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:46.967
And then when the migration of
SAP R/3 to SAP S/4HANA came up,

00:29:46.967 --> 00:29:48.550
we obviously asked
the question again.

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:51.250
Would it make sense now to
move into multi or hybrid cloud

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:53.440
environments, or
stay on premise,

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.850
or bring in even a second
public cloud provider?

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:58.390
But we made the decision,
OK, to stick with Google.

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.760
So therefore, we made the
decision also to move there

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:02.718
and obviously had some
discussions with Google.

00:30:02.718 --> 00:30:04.200
And we produced,
at the beginning,

00:30:04.200 --> 00:30:05.500
a couple of proof of concepts.

00:30:05.500 --> 00:30:07.300
And our people were
pretty happy about that--

00:30:07.300 --> 00:30:08.383
actually, very surprising.

00:30:08.383 --> 00:30:10.600
Because initially from
our internal people,

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.910
there was more a
pushback when we

00:30:12.910 --> 00:30:17.055
said, OK, we move to a public
cloud also on SAP space now.

00:30:17.055 --> 00:30:18.430
Because the feedback
was, OK, now

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:21.340
we want to have our own boxes
and everything under control.

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:24.310
But as I said, we started a
couple of proof of concepts.

00:30:24.310 --> 00:30:27.130
They were extremely easy.

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:28.880
And the initial proof
of concept initially

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:32.172
was planned to do it in two to
three months, and we did it--

00:30:32.172 --> 00:30:33.630
I don't know-- in
one or two weeks.

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:35.950
And that convinced, obviously,
also our tech people

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:36.985
and the SAP guys.

00:30:36.985 --> 00:30:38.860
And therefore, the
decision was pretty clear.

00:30:38.860 --> 00:30:41.770
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah, I think we
met, oh, at SAPPHIRE last year.

00:30:41.770 --> 00:30:42.720
So that was June.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:44.100
That was May last year.

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:46.635
And we said, hey, we
think we can do this.

00:30:46.635 --> 00:30:48.010
And I remember at
the time, we're

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:49.218
like, OK, let's give it a go.

00:30:49.218 --> 00:30:52.510
And yeah, I mean, 16 weeks
later, we concluded it all out

00:30:52.510 --> 00:30:54.040
and were proceeding forward.

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:56.270
I mean, that's lightning
speed in SAP world.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:58.750
But anyway, so in full
disclosure, I mean,

00:30:58.750 --> 00:31:00.370
these are massive
projects, right?

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:01.745
They're going to
go on for years.

00:31:01.745 --> 00:31:03.945
So just give us a sense on--

00:31:03.945 --> 00:31:06.070
so in your case, you've
been running now for, like,

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:06.790
six months?

00:31:06.790 --> 00:31:08.410
And you've been
running for six weeks,

00:31:08.410 --> 00:31:10.690
basically, effectively,
since we've

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:11.968
put the first boxes to use.

00:31:11.968 --> 00:31:14.260
I mean, just want to give us
an update on where you are

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:15.210
and what you've learned so far?

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:16.127
TIMO SALZSIEDER: Yeah.

00:31:16.127 --> 00:31:18.470
So the technology actually
is not the problem.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:20.188
It's more to consolidate
and harmonize

00:31:20.188 --> 00:31:22.480
the different processes across
the different countries.

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:24.910
So we spent quite a lot
of time convincing people

00:31:24.910 --> 00:31:26.467
and come to a standard approach.

00:31:26.467 --> 00:31:28.300
Because obviously, you
have a couple of CFOs

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:30.040
from the countries that want
to have a different setup

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:31.490
of the SAP environment.

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:33.610
So therefore, a lot of
conceptual work convincing

00:31:33.610 --> 00:31:35.110
these guys that it
makes sense to go

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:36.760
into one standard
approach, which,

00:31:36.760 --> 00:31:38.920
obviously, huge implications
on the processes

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:40.570
in the individual countries.

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:42.700
But there we are, and
we're in the middle

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:44.193
of the implementation right now.

00:31:44.193 --> 00:31:46.360
We're doing-- and this is
historically within Metro.

00:31:46.360 --> 00:31:48.068
We're doing a lot of
the stuff ourselves.

00:31:48.068 --> 00:31:50.560
So we're building up now
huge resources in India

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:54.640
to customize and
implement SAP S/4HANA.

00:31:54.640 --> 00:31:55.860
And this is ongoing.

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.360
And we are hiring, every couple
of days, a couple of people,

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:01.630
and just make sure that we have
a good SAP practice in-house

00:32:01.630 --> 00:32:04.207
and using not too many
partners to do this,

00:32:04.207 --> 00:32:06.040
but obviously, get a
lot of support from SAP

00:32:06.040 --> 00:32:07.882
directly, and also
obviously from Google.

00:32:07.882 --> 00:32:08.590
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:09.685
Meena?

00:32:09.685 --> 00:32:11.810
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: So
I think, as you mentioned,

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:15.080
our journey is very recent.

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:17.630
In terms of actually
process, I think

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:21.800
we have good alignment
from the standardization

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:23.630
and harmonization perspective.

00:32:23.630 --> 00:32:26.150
But that's not happened
overnight either,

00:32:26.150 --> 00:32:27.950
so that's been a
long journey forward.

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.120
PAUL YOUNG: Your
journey started way

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.020
before you started
talking to us.

00:32:30.020 --> 00:32:30.680
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
Way before, yeah.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:31.700
Way, way before.

00:32:31.700 --> 00:32:34.400
Actually, it started, I
think, three or four years ago

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:36.230
in Europe.

00:32:36.230 --> 00:32:40.190
So where they started the
implementation of ECC,

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:41.900
that was the intent, that--

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:43.580
and that started in Norway.

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:45.020
And that just
completed, I think,

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:47.600
sometime mid of last year.

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:51.300
And then it was paused, and then
the pivot to S/4 over there.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:53.660
So from a process
perspective, I think

00:32:53.660 --> 00:32:58.670
we were good when we started our
technical evaluation for where

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:01.170
it's going to be deployed.

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:03.170
So yeah, so it is very recent.

00:33:03.170 --> 00:33:07.550
So far, I think
the speed at which

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:11.600
we've been able to deploy even
the development environments--

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:12.440
they're non-sandbox.

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:15.830
They're actually
development environments.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:18.920
Our timeline was very
aggressive that was given to us,

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:22.700
considering that we
did a POC with Google,

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:26.210
concluded that
only last November.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:31.490
And we were told that all
the entire development stack

00:33:31.490 --> 00:33:34.760
and systems have
to be built by May.

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:37.440
So that was a pretty tall order.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:40.910
But so far, I think with
the partnership with Google,

00:33:40.910 --> 00:33:44.270
all the engineering teams
that have been brought in

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:47.480
to work with our internal
teams to enable the platform

00:33:47.480 --> 00:33:50.600
and get our deployments
going, I think

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:52.490
that's been an amazing
experience for us.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:57.140
And the ability to
actually upskill

00:33:57.140 --> 00:34:00.710
some of the teams that
have been working that's

00:34:00.710 --> 00:34:02.850
going to be actually--
so the independence,

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:06.300
the self-reliance aspect of
it is also very important.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:13.520
So that's been also achieved
in the short duration.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:16.460
So overall, I would
say so far, so good.

00:34:16.460 --> 00:34:17.147
Time will tell.

00:34:17.147 --> 00:34:18.980
PAUL YOUNG: I'm going
to ask Timo a question

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:20.730
and come back to that
point you just made.

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.510
But Timo, I've heard you
say this a couple of times,

00:34:23.510 --> 00:34:25.635
so I think it's fascinating
for the audience, which

00:34:25.635 --> 00:34:29.534
is the part of this for you
isn't just in the technology

00:34:29.534 --> 00:34:31.159
changes [INAUDIBLE],,
but it's actually

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:33.560
changing the culture at Metro.

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:35.239
And partnering with
Google was as much

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:36.844
of a change in the culture
as it was the technology.

00:34:36.844 --> 00:34:38.193
Do you want to explain that?

00:34:38.193 --> 00:34:39.110
TIMO SALZSIEDER: Sure.

00:34:39.110 --> 00:34:42.377
So like most others
probably also in the room,

00:34:42.377 --> 00:34:43.710
we have this [INAUDIBLE] talent.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.340
So we're really looking
actively for a lot of people.

00:34:46.340 --> 00:34:49.880
And we started our digital
journey with a lot of externals

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:51.560
helping us to build
up these platforms.

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:53.929
And we're now in the middle
of internalizing that.

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:56.113
And if you have people
in the organization

00:34:56.113 --> 00:34:57.530
that are working
for Metro since--

00:34:57.530 --> 00:34:59.113
I don't know--
20, 30, 40 years--

00:34:59.113 --> 00:35:01.280
and we really have people
working more than 40 years

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:03.800
for us, which is already a
bit strange to me, at least.

00:35:03.800 --> 00:35:06.320
But you talk to these people.

00:35:06.320 --> 00:35:09.080
And obviously, you see
that they are, well,

00:35:09.080 --> 00:35:10.730
fixed on a specific mindset.

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:12.645
And to change this,
it's obviously

00:35:12.645 --> 00:35:14.770
good to have an impact also
from the outside world.

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:17.295
So we get this push a bit
also from people like Google.

00:35:17.295 --> 00:35:19.670
So therefore, we're sending
people to the Google offices.

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:21.682
We try to do workshop together.

00:35:21.682 --> 00:35:23.390
We're running a couple
of innovation labs

00:35:23.390 --> 00:35:24.470
currently with Google.

00:35:24.470 --> 00:35:25.845
So therefore, this
helps us a lot

00:35:25.845 --> 00:35:27.980
in at least driving
that space a bit

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:30.385
into that innovation direction.

00:35:30.385 --> 00:35:32.510
But when it comes to culture,
it's a bit even more.

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:35.690
So we even did a rebranding
of our IT subsidiaries.

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:38.780
So it was formally called Metro
Systems, which is obviously not

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:40.760
that sexy, now called Metronom.

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:43.040
And we combined a
couple of organizations

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:44.150
into that organization.

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:47.990
And if you walk across our
campus at headquarters,

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:50.700
you see a lot of people
wearing Metronom hoodies

00:35:50.700 --> 00:35:51.750
and T-shirts, et cetera.

00:35:51.750 --> 00:35:54.020
And that was, two or three
years ago, impossible.

00:35:54.020 --> 00:35:55.895
And therefore, we are
pushing very, very hard

00:35:55.895 --> 00:35:56.900
on brand experience.

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.180
So we want to be just
a good employer brand,

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:01.620
and we are very
aggressive on that topic.

00:36:01.620 --> 00:36:03.680
I do a lot of internal
service with our people.

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:05.513
And actually, we do
this on a monthly basis,

00:36:05.513 --> 00:36:07.020
so not on an annual
survey basis,

00:36:07.020 --> 00:36:09.650
just to figure out how well
we're treating the people,

00:36:09.650 --> 00:36:11.300
and we're investing quite a lot.

00:36:11.300 --> 00:36:14.245
And in terms of that,
obviously, a partner like Google

00:36:14.245 --> 00:36:14.870
helps us a lot.

00:36:14.870 --> 00:36:16.245
Because I mean,
I think Google is

00:36:16.245 --> 00:36:18.380
known for having an
exceptional culture.

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:21.472
And we don't want to
copy the Google culture.

00:36:21.472 --> 00:36:23.180
I think that's impossible
with our setup.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:25.070
But at least, there are
some things to learn.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:27.195
And also, when it comes to
hiring processes and all

00:36:27.195 --> 00:36:29.180
these HR processes,
we're just looking

00:36:29.180 --> 00:36:30.200
at what Google is doing.

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:32.015
And there's a very
strong relationship.

00:36:32.015 --> 00:36:33.890
And what we're doing
now, since about a year,

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:37.190
is we are introducing objectives
and key results, OKRs, which

00:36:37.190 --> 00:36:39.090
is pretty famous at Google.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:41.460
And I think if you talk
to the founders of Google,

00:36:41.460 --> 00:36:43.700
they at least tell
you that OKRs was

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:45.800
one of the key
principles at Google

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:47.040
to drive this organization.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:48.350
So there is a lot of
exchange, and we're

00:36:48.350 --> 00:36:49.550
learning a lot from Google.

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:51.860
And therefore, it's
more about, yeah,

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:53.960
exchanging culture,
exchanging processes,

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:56.530
rather than only having a
technology collaboration.

00:36:56.530 --> 00:36:57.410
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah, I think
one of things you said

00:36:57.410 --> 00:36:59.630
was that basically, every
time-- the Google guys

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:00.290
are sitting in the audience.

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:01.850
But every time the
Google guys came

00:37:01.850 --> 00:37:03.517
to visit Metro during
the sales process,

00:37:03.517 --> 00:37:05.475
they were always wearing
Google T-shirts, which

00:37:05.475 --> 00:37:07.970
is one of the things that
really resonated for Metro,

00:37:07.970 --> 00:37:10.290
is that they were very
proud of their brand

00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:11.540
and they wanted to display it.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:14.500
And then your own people
started to replicate that.

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:16.250
And it's a really
interesting conversation

00:37:16.250 --> 00:37:18.290
because we were
having this yesterday.

00:37:18.290 --> 00:37:22.250
I mean, anything you do with
SAP is hopefully a long-term

00:37:22.250 --> 00:37:23.930
marriage, right--

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:24.742
multiple ways.

00:37:24.742 --> 00:37:25.450
There's partners.

00:37:25.450 --> 00:37:25.730
There's SAP.

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:26.030
There's us.

00:37:26.030 --> 00:37:27.050
There's everything
else involved.

00:37:27.050 --> 00:37:28.967
So on the one hand, it's
a long-term marriage.

00:37:28.967 --> 00:37:31.220
On the other hand, I
mean, ultimately, you

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.950
want us to be electricity
service, right?

00:37:33.950 --> 00:37:36.572
I mean, ultimately, you want
to go to SAP and turn it on,

00:37:36.572 --> 00:37:38.780
and it works, and it updates,
and it does everything.

00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:41.348
And so it's a little
bit of a challenge,

00:37:41.348 --> 00:37:43.640
frankly, for me as we look
at the business growing out,

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:45.567
which is, what do you
really want from us?

00:37:45.567 --> 00:37:47.150
Because on the one
hand, what you want

00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:48.980
is stability and,
effectively, never

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:51.457
to have to talk to us
because the stuff just runs.

00:37:51.457 --> 00:37:53.540
On the other hand, we do
have to maintain contact,

00:37:53.540 --> 00:37:55.470
and we do have to grow together.

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:57.867
And so if you have a
concept, I'm interested.

00:37:57.867 --> 00:37:59.450
But it's a long-term
challenge for me,

00:37:59.450 --> 00:38:05.120
which is, after
the sale is done,

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:06.770
what is it you
actually want from us

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:08.930
in a long-term
relationship to make SAP

00:38:08.930 --> 00:38:10.280
successful in the long term?

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:12.113
TIMO SALZSIEDER: I think
you just nailed it.

00:38:12.113 --> 00:38:14.690
Because we consider, at
least in that context--

00:38:14.690 --> 00:38:16.220
when I talk about
digital and I'm

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:17.992
talking about
warehouse management,

00:38:17.992 --> 00:38:19.700
I think it's a different
topic, or supply

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:21.290
chain as other examples.

00:38:21.290 --> 00:38:23.960
But when it comes to financial
services and financial

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:26.230
processes internally,
it simply needs to run.

00:38:26.230 --> 00:38:27.480
It needs to be up and running.

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:29.310
So therefore, the
example you just

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:31.040
brought with electricity
or water, that's

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:34.200
what we're expecting from you--
is making sure that this works,

00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:35.930
and that it runs
without any hassle,

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:37.513
and that it provides
good scalability,

00:38:37.513 --> 00:38:39.763
that security is fine so
that nobody is stealing stuff

00:38:39.763 --> 00:38:40.490
from us.

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:43.170
So that's our requirement
at this point.

00:38:43.170 --> 00:38:44.570
But as I said,
long-term, I could

00:38:44.570 --> 00:38:48.080
imagine that we're driving this
relationship between Google,

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:50.420
SAP, and Metro even
to the next level.

00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:52.310
And then we have a
bit of more demands,

00:38:52.310 --> 00:38:54.350
because then it comes
into innovation;

00:38:54.350 --> 00:38:56.000
that we are talking
about new things

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:57.650
that we deploy to
specific countries;

00:38:57.650 --> 00:39:00.247
that we have also, on this
cultural level, some exchange.

00:39:00.247 --> 00:39:01.580
So this is what we're expecting.

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:05.180
But at this point when it comes
to the finance applications,

00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:07.890
it's all about making sure
that it works in a proper way,

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:09.980
and that it runs smooth
for our customers.

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:11.790
And I'm talking about
internal customers.

00:39:11.790 --> 00:39:12.800
So therefore, I
just simply don't

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:15.050
want to get any stress with
the CFOs in the countries.

00:39:15.050 --> 00:39:16.467
I just want to
hear from them in--

00:39:16.467 --> 00:39:18.133
I don't know-- one
or two or three years

00:39:18.133 --> 00:39:19.600
when we're done
with that process,

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:21.438
and that it works
smooth, and that they

00:39:21.438 --> 00:39:22.730
don't have any problems at all.

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:26.060
Because it's good, as a CIO,
that if you hear nothing,

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:26.810
then it's perfect.

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:28.580
And that's what I
would like to achieve.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:30.413
PAUL YOUNG: And Meena,
I think you equally--

00:39:30.413 --> 00:39:32.800
I mean, we're at the start
of the journey with McKesson.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:35.390
But obviously, you and me were
[INAUDIBLE] five weeks ago.

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:39.110
And we started this process
where basically, the road

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:41.540
track for you is we did
a hackathon, basically,

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:43.430
where we had our team
and your team together.

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:44.222
It was fascinating.

00:39:44.222 --> 00:39:47.720
I mean, literally, I think,
three hours into the hackathon,

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:50.220
my team was elbowed out of the
way, and your guys took over.

00:39:50.220 --> 00:39:52.200
And it was an amazing process.

00:39:52.200 --> 00:39:56.027
But again, how are
you going to build,

00:39:56.027 --> 00:39:58.610
I guess, internally at McKesson
to get the transitional change

00:39:58.610 --> 00:39:59.110
to happen?

00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:01.230
And then, how do you
think the long term works?

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:03.230
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
Yeah, I think essentially,

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:04.880
that was the start
of it, the hackathon

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:11.960
that you talked about, just
to get that base excited.

00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:15.050
It's an amazing thing to
watch when they actually

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:18.890
get to do this themselves.

00:40:18.890 --> 00:40:21.020
Literally, even
after that week, I

00:40:21.020 --> 00:40:24.380
think there's so
much of feedback

00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:27.890
I've gotten from that team that
they felt so empowered just

00:40:27.890 --> 00:40:31.190
going through that process
and being able to actually--

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:37.843
SAP, I know it can be pretty
mundane in terms of technology

00:40:37.843 --> 00:40:38.510
and all of that.

00:40:38.510 --> 00:40:43.730
It's pretty-- it
has a long history.

00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:47.900
But just to be able to use
the cloud capabilities to be

00:40:47.900 --> 00:40:50.540
able to build some
level of automation

00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:52.130
that we built even
that weekend, we

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:56.390
continued to hone
on more and more.

00:40:56.390 --> 00:40:58.730
I think that is
exciting people there.

00:40:58.730 --> 00:41:03.320
And also on top of that now,
our go-forward strategy is,

00:41:03.320 --> 00:41:06.680
let SAP do what it's best for.

00:41:06.680 --> 00:41:12.620
And then anything else, anything
differentiating capabilities

00:41:12.620 --> 00:41:16.730
that you want to build for
your competitive advantage

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:18.960
in the market, take it out.

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:19.830
Don't build there.

00:41:19.830 --> 00:41:23.810
And so that is also going to
give the teams an opportunity

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:26.210
to actually learn new skills.

00:41:26.210 --> 00:41:28.730
And they may not become
developers overnight

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:29.860
and use the--

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:33.360
but there is definitely
a desire there

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:35.477
when you start to
paint that picture.

00:41:35.477 --> 00:41:37.310
So you have to give
them those opportunities

00:41:37.310 --> 00:41:39.990
to be able to skill as well.

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:42.560
And that's what we are
focused on as well.

00:41:42.560 --> 00:41:46.320
There are a lot of teams
that do that type of work,

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:49.110
so we are trying to see
where there is interest

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:52.490
within the existing teams
and help them get plugged

00:41:52.490 --> 00:41:56.810
into those groups and get
some experience working

00:41:56.810 --> 00:41:59.570
on some of those projects.

00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:03.470
The other thing is what
we've been doing also,

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:07.160
almost like a "Shark
Tank" kind of concept

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:10.280
where we have been
letting people

00:42:10.280 --> 00:42:12.320
pitch some ideas
about what they want

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:16.320
to do on the cloud platform.

00:42:16.320 --> 00:42:21.320
And of course, it has to
have some value that we are

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:23.300
going to eventually deliver.

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:25.970
But those are the
ideas we are getting

00:42:25.970 --> 00:42:27.650
from people and from the teams.

00:42:27.650 --> 00:42:31.010
And then we are picking a
couple to invest in and let

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:31.940
them work on it.

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:37.670
So I mean, all of this is really
helping build that excitement.

00:42:37.670 --> 00:42:43.170
And obviously, then it attracts
external talent as well.

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:46.010
And so yeah, so
there's a lot of things

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:49.960
we are trying to do to
energize the teams there.

00:42:49.960 --> 00:42:50.960
PAUL YOUNG: Very cool.

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:52.970
So I guess two final
questions, one of which

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:56.670
is incredibly dangerous
because, A, there's

00:42:56.670 --> 00:42:58.670
a camera in the back of
the room recording this.

00:42:58.670 --> 00:43:01.620
And B, I'm going to ask the
question that the general rule

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:03.120
say, never ask this question.

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:05.205
But for me personally,
one of my personal things

00:43:05.205 --> 00:43:07.080
always is when I get
feedback, is like, yeah,

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:08.060
I like the nice things.

00:43:08.060 --> 00:43:09.620
Tell me what we
need to do better.

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:10.820
It's all about improvement.

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:12.650
And actually, one of my team
actually recently asked me

00:43:12.650 --> 00:43:13.190
how they were doing.

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:13.910
I was like, you're doing great.

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:15.120
And they're, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:16.745
Tell me about what
I need to do better.

00:43:16.745 --> 00:43:18.560
I'm like, OK, I
like that attitude.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:22.400
In that spirit, what do
you need us to do better?

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:25.840
What can we do more for
you as we go forward here?

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:27.900
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
So I think--

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.870
well, our journey is recent.

00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:31.673
So I think we'll
continue to discover--

00:43:31.673 --> 00:43:33.090
PAUL YOUNG: You'll
get back to me?

00:43:33.090 --> 00:43:33.850
[LAUGHS]

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:34.975
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:43:34.975 --> 00:43:39.150
But I think our goal is really
to learn from your teams,

00:43:39.150 --> 00:43:41.820
and then get--

00:43:41.820 --> 00:43:45.510
if not completely,
but have a fair amount

00:43:45.510 --> 00:43:47.080
of self-independence.

00:43:47.080 --> 00:43:51.300
So we want Google to take
that approach with us as well,

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:55.380
focus more on how we can learn.

00:43:55.380 --> 00:43:58.950
And so that's one thing.

00:43:58.950 --> 00:44:03.600
And then continue to
challenge our mindset,

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:04.560
continue to do that.

00:44:04.560 --> 00:44:08.320
Because sometimes, we can
be fixated in some things.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:10.080
And so continue to do that.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:15.550
And for me, that's been
something I've seen

00:44:15.550 --> 00:44:17.440
and I hope to continue to see.

00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:18.300
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah,
one of the feedbacks

00:44:18.300 --> 00:44:19.680
we've heard loud and
hard from customers

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:21.960
is we need to start doing
customer panels and customer

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:23.680
get-togethers,
and start sharing.

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:26.100
And driving that community
and knowledge better

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:27.140
is clearly one of the
things we have to do.

00:44:27.140 --> 00:44:27.640
I got it.

00:44:27.640 --> 00:44:28.260
Timo?

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:29.430
I'm sure you have feedback.

00:44:29.430 --> 00:44:30.150
TIMO SALZSIEDER: Yeah.

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:31.520
PAUL YOUNG: And you get the
chance to speak it out loud,

00:44:31.520 --> 00:44:31.690
so--

00:44:31.690 --> 00:44:32.690
TIMO SALZSIEDER:
Yeah, that's true.

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:35.490
So I could repeat a couple of
things that you just mentioned.

00:44:35.490 --> 00:44:38.520
But one topic we would
like to see a bit more

00:44:38.520 --> 00:44:39.960
is more business talk.

00:44:39.960 --> 00:44:41.765
Because, I mean,
at the end, Google

00:44:41.765 --> 00:44:44.140
is a very engineering-driven
organization, which is fine.

00:44:44.140 --> 00:44:46.020
And actually, our engineers
love the way Google

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:46.770
is doing business.

00:44:46.770 --> 00:44:48.760
But now from a top
management perspective,

00:44:48.760 --> 00:44:51.618
it would be just nice to
get a bit more support

00:44:51.618 --> 00:44:53.160
and a bit more
insights when it comes

00:44:53.160 --> 00:44:55.020
to business-related functions.

00:44:55.020 --> 00:44:57.000
So therefore, we
highly appreciate

00:44:57.000 --> 00:45:00.660
what just has been communicated
in the keynote meetings

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:03.480
yesterday and today, these
industry-specific solutions

00:45:03.480 --> 00:45:06.072
that will be developed by
Google and by partners.

00:45:06.072 --> 00:45:08.280
So this is something we're
really looking forward to,

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:09.930
because as I said,
our business is not

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:11.130
a classical retail business.

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.530
So we're really looking
to get more innovation

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:15.100
into our organization,
et cetera.

00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:17.520
And therefore, we're expecting,
from a strategic partner

00:45:17.520 --> 00:45:20.550
like Google and SAP, that
we're getting a bit more

00:45:20.550 --> 00:45:22.080
feedback and a bit
more consultancy

00:45:22.080 --> 00:45:24.420
when it comes to
business-specific questions we

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:25.055
might have.

00:45:25.055 --> 00:45:26.430
And also what you
just mentioned,

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:29.130
customer panels, et cetera,
to exchange with other peers

00:45:29.130 --> 00:45:30.690
would be very helpful.

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:33.510
But, well, building
industry-specific solutions

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:36.510
for the retail industry that
fit also our specific needs,

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:37.680
that would be just perfect.

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:40.320
Because so far,
excellent technology,

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:41.320
no question about that.

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:44.390
And I as, also, a
computer science guy,

00:45:44.390 --> 00:45:46.830
appreciate that in keynotes,
there are some unique shells

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:48.480
and you see some
commands typed in.

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:50.730
But obviously, I would like
to see a bit more business

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:52.430
talk in the future, and
that's our requirement.

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:53.160
PAUL YOUNG: Cool.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:55.410
And so clock is running down.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:56.100
Last question.

00:45:59.060 --> 00:45:59.900
You're on a journey.

00:45:59.900 --> 00:46:00.930
There's a lot of people
sitting in the audience who

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:02.513
are probably thinking,
yeah, I'm going

00:46:02.513 --> 00:46:03.980
to have to do the same thing.

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:05.870
There's some people in this
audience who are thinking,

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:06.950
I want to do the same thing.

00:46:06.950 --> 00:46:08.420
And there's some people in
the audience who are thinking,

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:10.295
I have to do the same
thing whether I want it

00:46:10.295 --> 00:46:11.630
or not, right?

00:46:11.630 --> 00:46:13.580
Knowing what you
know now, what's

00:46:13.580 --> 00:46:15.167
your best advice to them?

00:46:15.167 --> 00:46:17.000
TIMO SALZSIEDER: It's
actually quite simple.

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:19.550
So just make sure that you
take the team with you.

00:46:19.550 --> 00:46:20.990
That would be my major advice.

00:46:20.990 --> 00:46:22.760
So it was not a
top-down decision.

00:46:22.760 --> 00:46:25.550
It was, OK, let's move into
the Google Cloud environment.

00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:26.717
We really asked our people.

00:46:26.717 --> 00:46:27.800
We got really some buy-in.

00:46:27.800 --> 00:46:29.083
We built a lot of prototypes.

00:46:29.083 --> 00:46:30.750
So if you have the
buy-in from your team

00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:33.292
and your organization, then it's
actually a fairly easy move.

00:46:33.292 --> 00:46:35.100
But if you do it
top-down and say, OK--

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:36.080
I mean, I don't know.

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:38.105
If everybody would be
a huge Microsoft fan,

00:46:38.105 --> 00:46:39.980
and then we make the
decision towards Google,

00:46:39.980 --> 00:46:41.230
then this would be a disaster.

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:43.670
But in our case, we simply
informed the people up front.

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:45.170
We asked for their
opinion, and just

00:46:45.170 --> 00:46:47.253
made sure that we have a
lot of proof of concepts,

00:46:47.253 --> 00:46:49.070
and just got the
buy-in from the team.

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:50.720
And if you have that,
then it's an easy going.

00:46:50.720 --> 00:46:51.710
So I mean, we're
not through yet.

00:46:51.710 --> 00:46:53.752
So I'm expecting, obviously,
over the next couple

00:46:53.752 --> 00:46:56.300
of months and years, some
issues within the project, which

00:46:56.300 --> 00:46:57.530
is pretty normal.

00:46:57.530 --> 00:46:59.720
But I know that the team
is highly motivated.

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:02.277
And I know, when we first
communicated in the SAP space

00:47:02.277 --> 00:47:04.610
that we're moving towards
cloud, we got a huge pushback.

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:06.560
But then when the guys
saw the technology

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:08.330
and could play around
with the technology,

00:47:08.330 --> 00:47:09.497
it was fairly easy for them.

00:47:09.497 --> 00:47:11.120
And therefore, we
have now, well,

00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:14.450
transformed basically our
SAP guys from on-premise fans

00:47:14.450 --> 00:47:15.230
into Google fans.

00:47:15.230 --> 00:47:17.710
And this obviously helps
to run such a huge project.

00:47:17.710 --> 00:47:18.470
PAUL YOUNG: Cool.

00:47:18.470 --> 00:47:20.070
Meena?

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:22.910
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
So I think very similar.

00:47:22.910 --> 00:47:28.190
So for us, it had to be a little
bit of a top-down as well.

00:47:28.190 --> 00:47:32.780
Because I think that's
also needed because people

00:47:32.780 --> 00:47:35.600
are looking for that
prescriptive direction

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:36.800
as well at times.

00:47:36.800 --> 00:47:37.760
So that's helped.

00:47:37.760 --> 00:47:39.770
So it's both ways--
energizing our--

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:42.050
it's the bottom-up and
top-down approach that's

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:43.670
really worked well for us.

00:47:43.670 --> 00:47:48.410
And then the other thing
is, don't think too much.

00:47:48.410 --> 00:47:50.120
Get started.

00:47:50.120 --> 00:47:53.060
And it's OK to fail fast.

00:47:53.060 --> 00:47:56.120
Try things out, and you will
not know until you actually

00:47:56.120 --> 00:47:57.560
try things out.

00:47:57.560 --> 00:47:59.330
And there's a lot to learn.

00:47:59.330 --> 00:48:04.520
And like I mentioned, McKesson
has been on this cloud journey

00:48:04.520 --> 00:48:05.630
for a while.

00:48:05.630 --> 00:48:08.550
But it will take some time.

00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:16.490
So think less, start, but
then go slow in terms of you

00:48:16.490 --> 00:48:18.050
have to really pace
it out in terms

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:20.010
of what you want to achieve.

00:48:20.010 --> 00:48:25.238
So I think that's at least what
I see has been working for us.

00:48:25.238 --> 00:48:26.030
PAUL YOUNG: Thanks.

00:48:26.030 --> 00:48:28.200
I'll tell you, one
of the things for me

00:48:28.200 --> 00:48:31.550
is we are 30-second provisioning
globally for any SAP system.

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:35.090
It's all per-second billing, all
the way up to our entire fleet.

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:38.303
So unlike a classic SAP
project where you start

00:48:38.303 --> 00:48:39.470
thinking about it this year.

00:48:39.470 --> 00:48:40.460
You size it out.

00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:41.420
You get sticker shock.

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:42.462
You put it in the budget.

00:48:42.462 --> 00:48:44.660
You maybe do it next year.

00:48:44.660 --> 00:48:47.470
You can do this this afternoon
on a credit card, right?

00:48:47.470 --> 00:48:53.100
And it's that-- adjusting to the
removement of the restrictions

00:48:53.100 --> 00:48:54.250
is kind of hard, right?

00:48:54.250 --> 00:48:56.380
I think right back at the
start the conversation.

00:48:56.380 --> 00:48:57.840
It's not about the technology.

00:48:57.840 --> 00:48:59.010
It's about the process.

00:48:59.010 --> 00:48:59.920
And once you understand
what's possible,

00:48:59.920 --> 00:49:00.837
you can go from there.

00:49:00.837 --> 00:49:03.500
Hey, thank you for your time,
for spending the time with us

00:49:03.500 --> 00:49:04.410
this afternoon.

00:49:04.410 --> 00:49:06.463
Timo, Meena,
sincerely appreciate

00:49:06.463 --> 00:49:07.880
you giving this
insight and wisdom

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:09.130
and spending the time with us.

00:49:09.130 --> 00:49:11.140
Really appreciate it.

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:14.030
Like I said, you can
go this afternoon

00:49:14.030 --> 00:49:15.840
and start an SAP
server in 30 seconds.

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:19.107
That's never been
done before at scale.

00:49:19.107 --> 00:49:20.690
There's no reason
not to give it a go.

00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:23.315
In the meantime, I will tell you
that the next organizers would

00:49:23.315 --> 00:49:25.302
dearly appreciate if you
would provide feedback

00:49:25.302 --> 00:49:27.010
on this session so we
know what you liked

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:29.450
and what you didn't like and
what we should do next year.

00:49:29.450 --> 00:49:32.380
But at our rate of growth at
SAP, I think there'll be a few

00:49:32.380 --> 00:49:35.090
more of these to come
as we go forward.

00:49:35.090 --> 00:49:36.590
So thanks for your time.

00:49:36.590 --> 00:49:38.590
And my last call-- actually,
I have to say this.

00:49:38.590 --> 00:49:41.068
If you're interested
at all, why wait?

00:49:41.068 --> 00:49:43.110
Yesterday, Andy and Sidney,
we had a conversation

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:43.470
with Andy and Sidney.

00:49:43.470 --> 00:49:45.590
And when I asked Andy
a similar question,

00:49:45.590 --> 00:49:49.120
his answer was, tell me exactly
what your plan B is, right?

00:49:49.120 --> 00:49:53.850
Because if plan A is wait
and see if I need to do this,

00:49:53.850 --> 00:49:56.660
at some point, you're going
to have to change, right?

00:49:56.660 --> 00:49:59.440
And so if it is this simple
and it is this possible,

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:01.825
why are we all waiting?

00:50:01.825 --> 00:50:03.950
Competitive advantage is
on your side if you do it.

00:50:03.950 --> 00:50:06.482
And if you want to
do that, call me.

00:50:06.482 --> 00:50:08.440
We'll more than happily
come out and visit you.

00:50:08.440 --> 00:50:09.430
But thank you all.

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:11.330
And Meena and Timo,
thank you one more time.

00:50:11.330 --> 00:50:11.830
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:11.830 --> 00:50:15.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

